00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.350
This is the Palmer
Penguin's data set.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.950
If you're unfamiliar this is a
fairly kind of recent to the R

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.009
space data set, you can
find some more information

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.140
at Alison Horst’s GitHub
repository that I

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.963
have linked here in the slides.

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:15.380
But basically, the
idea here is we

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.440
have 344 different
observations for three

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.900
different penguin
species: Chinstrap, Gentoo

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.620
and this Adelie.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.300
And if you're familiar
with the iris data set

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.850
that the typical kind
of classification data

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.390
set you often see this is
a similar style data set.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.880
Right fairly easy to
understand down here

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.850
on the bottom right
hand corner, you

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.050
can see kind of a preview
what the data looks like.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.250
We've got the species
that island they came from.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.470
Do we have some
different measurements

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.060
from each of these
different penguins.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.370
Their bill length, bill
depth, flipper length, body

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.355
mass…

00:02:47.355 --> 00:02:49.730
And then we have the sex of
the penguin and then the year

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.063
that the measurement took place.

00:02:51.063 --> 00:02:52.610
So what we want
to do here is just

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.943
kind of frame this and this
could be any data set right.

00:02:54.943 --> 00:02:57.050
This is the example that
we walk through today

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.898
is not specific to this
data set by any stretch

00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:00.690
of the imagination.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.170
But I think like
most data science

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.360
most analytical problems or
projects start with some data

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.620
and that felt like a natural
place to start today.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.440
Let's start with some
data and go from there.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.720
So what we want to
do now is we will

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.420
say we've been handed this data,
and we're going to go ahead

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.350
and we're going to do the
data science or in some cases,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.110
you know means build
the model, and we're

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.780
going to take these
three or 44 observations.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.870
This is not a model building or
necessarily data science themed

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:32.370
webinar.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.590
So this is not about how to
build the best model possible.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.190
This is just let's work with an
example that that's easy for us

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:37.773
to understand.

00:03:37.773 --> 00:03:41.600
So we'll take the data you know
we might explore it clean it

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.420
up a little bit.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.920
And then we'll fit
this model to it.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.970
And the idea here is given
some set of measurements.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.700
So given the bill
length, the bill depth,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.540
the flipper length
and the body mass,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.997
we want to predict the species
of that particular penguin.

00:03:54.997 --> 00:03:57.080
Right and so that's what
we've done in the example

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.455
that we have pulled
up on screen.

00:03:58.455 --> 00:04:01.328
We fit this model using
the tidymodels framework

00:04:01.328 --> 00:04:03.620
and then we're able to see
some output from that model.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.870
And now that we have this, we
can generate new predictions

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:07.370
right.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.710
We can given a set
of new data that we

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.780
get a new set of
measurements from a penguin

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:12.860
of unknown species.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.110
And we want to predict what
the species of penguin is.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.810
We can pass those new
values through to the model

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.720
and generate a predicted
outcome in terms of what species

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.740
we think is that
penguin is likely to be.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.570
Now this is all great,
but the question now

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.640
is kind of, well, now what, right?

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.540
We we have a couple of options.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.460
Now now that we've
done our work.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.940
We've built this model, we
have a few options ahead of us.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.990
One is we can just
kind of ad hoc score

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.900
new penguin predictions
whenever we receive new data.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.140
Right So maybe once a month.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.250
We get an email from researchers
that are off researching

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.500
penguins and they send
us their new set of data

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.660
and we run that sort of
data through the model

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.780
in order to predict the species
of these new measurements.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.920
That works right.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.510
But maybe we want
something that's

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:57.420
a little bit more real time.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.180
Maybe there's maybe
measurements are happening

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.138
at a pretty rapid rate.

00:05:00.138 --> 00:05:03.230
And we don't want our
entire day job from here

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.110
on out to be predicting
penguin species using

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:07.713
the model that we built right.

00:05:07.713 --> 00:05:09.380
But we also don't
want the model that we

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.130
built to just kind of
wither away and die.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.620
We spend a lot of time here.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.780
This was something that
we feel like contributes

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.010
to the goals of our business
or the goals of the project.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.020
We're working on.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.500
And we want to make sure that
it continues to contribute

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.120
in the best way possible.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.990
But the problem is we're kind
of stuck because we either

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:28.370
have to be the
ones that generate

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.550
new values and new
predictions because we're

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:31.800
the ones that built the model.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.950
And we know how to
use R or we have

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.560
to find somebody else
that knows are and can

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:37.760
do that themselves right.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.340
So we maybe we email
them or we provide them

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.840
with access to the
model and they're

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.590
able to generate
their own predictions.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.110
But that's still
kind of a high bar

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.818
because that means
that that person needs

00:05:46.818 --> 00:05:49.880
to be able to open up an R
session and load our model

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.640
and load the data and run the
data through the model, which

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.890
if you're an R
user that's probably

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:55.980
not that big of a deal.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.150
But if you're trying to teach
one of the business users

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.133
or one of end users of
your model how to run this

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:04.550
and you've got to teach them
about are now all the sudden,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.790
this becomes a much larger task.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.400
The other option is we
could just pass this off

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.020
to somebody else we could
say, OK, we've done our job.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:12.770
Here's the model.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:14.600
Here's kind of how
we set this up.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.960
Now let's let that model
grow up quote unquote.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.170
And we'll give it to
an engineering team

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.770
and they'll reconstruct
this model in a framework

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:25.070
that they're using.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.210
So that it can be used for
real time scoring or real time

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:33.620
predicting of new data and that
that's a totally valid option,

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.730
but it's often a little bit
tricky to get that right.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.590
Right to be able to hand
off the model in that way,

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.870
it's time consuming.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:43.630
It it can be a
difficult process to go

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.550
through when in
reality, we already

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:47.080
have everything we need.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.470
What we really need is just a
convenient way for other people

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:52.270
to be able to use our model.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.100
And that's really
where APIs come in.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:57.170
So let's take a step aside
from the model for a moment.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:00.490
And let's just talk about
this notion of what an APIs

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:05.290
and in general, the API stands
for Application Programming

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:06.790
Interface.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.720
Now that can mean a lot
of different things.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.900
And in different contexts,
the idea of an API

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.150
can mean different things,
which makes it a little bit

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.620
difficult to understand.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.650
And so for the purpose
of our conversation today

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.690
we're going to narrow
in on this definition

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:27.318
that we can get an APIs a web
API communicating over a HTTP.

00:07:27.318 --> 00:07:29.110
And basically what that
is that provides us

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.870
with a standardized
way for different can

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.360
for different computers to
communicate with one another.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:36.230
I get answer that.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.140
And I'm not going to dive
into the nitty gritty details

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.840
of what makes up an API request
and what a response looks like.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.940
There's lots of different
features involved here

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:44.710
that you can look
into and that and that

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.260
will provide some resources
that provide some detail there.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.240
But the main idea
is when we talk

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:53.170
about web APIs over
HTTP, which is what we're

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:54.850
going to talk about today.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:59.020
This is just a standardized
set way for different computers

00:07:59.020 --> 00:07:59.680
to communicate.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.100
Just like I'm communicating.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.000
Now through my voice.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.040
And then you're able
to understand computers

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:09.100
have their own way of
communicating HTTP web

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.130
APIs one of those mechanisms
for communication.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:14.140
The idea is there's
a client that

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:19.250
sends a request that request
meets the standard of what

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:20.850
the server is expecting.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.490
And then a server
receives that request

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:24.407
and then generates some
sort of a response.

00:08:24.407 --> 00:08:26.240
And that response can
be something as simple

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.190
as, I received the request.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:29.540
That's all that's all I need.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:31.332
That's all I need to
do to something that's

00:08:31.332 --> 00:08:34.010
much more complex like firing
off some sort of process

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.140
or updating a
database or generating

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.559
predictions against a model or
any number of different things

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:42.990
can happen in response to
a request that comes in,

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.380
but the server is responsible
for taking the request

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.560
and doing something with it.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.200
And then providing a response.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.710
And even if you've never really
interacted directly knowingly

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.832
with API in the past.

00:08:54.832 --> 00:08:56.540
And it's interesting
to note that anytime

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:58.200
you use a lot of web browsers.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.180
If you use Google Chrome
or Safari or Brave or Firefox

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:03.240
or anything like that.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.680
And you visit a website.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.640
There's a request
that your browser

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.420
makes to a server
that says, hey,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.120
I'd like to view this web page.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.290
That's that's at this address.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.470
And the server responds with
typically a bunch of html

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.470
that your browser, then
renders for you to view.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.390
So anytime you view
a website, you're

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:21.950
really interacting with API.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.530
Even if you don't
acknowledge that fact right.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.113
Your computer is a client
and it's sending a request.

00:09:27.113 --> 00:09:29.030
And there's a server
somewhere that responding

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:34.110
to that request with some email
that you view it as a web page.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:35.700
Now this is, again,
not meant to be

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.115
a comprehensive overview of
what APIs are or how APIs work.

00:09:40.115 --> 00:09:41.490
But this hopefully
sets the stage

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:43.190
and gives you a little
bit of background

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.470
and the next general question.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.880
I think that makes
sense to ask here is.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.330
OK, well, that's great.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:51.690
But as an R user
as a statistician

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.900
as an actuary as a data
scientist as an analyst

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.050
why do I care right.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.690
Why is this something
that's important to me.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:02.450
And I think there's
kind of two reasons

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.020
here that this
becomes significant.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.440
And it goes back to
our initial conundrum

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.060
where we built this model.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:11.147
And we want to give
people access to it.

00:10:11.147 --> 00:10:12.980
But we don't want to
train the whole company

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.260
on how to use our right.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.510
And the fact is
APIs allow the work

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:21.280
that you do to be used by a wide
range of tools and technologies

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:21.990
at that.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.220
And that that's no
longer no longer limited

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:28.280
to just the language.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.720
So I can now
essentially say, OK,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.062
I've got this model that
we're going to work with them

00:10:33.062 --> 00:10:34.270
will walk through an example.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:38.000
This is the moment
I this model here.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.990
And I've got another team in
my organization that's using

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:45.170
Python or they're using C
or they're using JavaScript

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:48.560
